[{:db/id "q-332659/field-1/80f3562bac7a231e2e20a34fb8e2c112b9c749a40182b489ca82a2363d61641c" :answer/id #uuid "56622ace-6fee-5f7e-9817-a3fb4b9d29eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}e^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332659/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f497ed-1344-5460-9b0a-6f4b2b8a164a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332659/field-1/80f3562bac7a231e2e20a34fb8e2c112b9c749a40182b489ca82a2363d61641c" :answer-field/choices ["q-332659/field-1/80f3562bac7a231e2e20a34fb8e2c112b9c749a40182b489ca82a2363d61641c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332659" :question/id #uuid "07b0d75a-b632-5a31-b7e2-32353413f5df" :question/math-academy-id "q-332659" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-18y=24{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $24{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})-18(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (-12-18)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -30\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332659/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332659"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332669/field-1/c1cf247148a7009d51510bd8e0188bb7539d51eac4fdf8b385bb7129afef1113" :answer/id #uuid "332865e6-9a5b-5d5c-b86b-117ec5da9654" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}e^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}e^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332669/field-1/869cf79cfda95970e8e60dbddba88a1d01ef43a4e7c43d49eb054e4d5d55da77" :answer/id #uuid "8e49c025-3cae-5a35-a78c-63f2aa273051" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}e^{2x}-\\frac{1}{8}e^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332669/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "313afa76-69ee-5070-9624-01f055352dba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332669/field-1/c1cf247148a7009d51510bd8e0188bb7539d51eac4fdf8b385bb7129afef1113" :answer-field/choices ["q-332669/field-1/c1cf247148a7009d51510bd8e0188bb7539d51eac4fdf8b385bb7129afef1113" "q-332669/field-1/869cf79cfda95970e8e60dbddba88a1d01ef43a4e7c43d49eb054e4d5d55da77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332669" :question/id #uuid "aba6e3a2-6b9c-5da4-a0fb-f7b0bbffa2ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-332669" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x},$ and neither ${e}^{2x}$ nor ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x}) & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ (2\\alpha +3\\alpha ){e}^{2x}+(5\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{2x}+8\\beta {e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{5x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n5\\alpha =2,\\,8\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{5x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332669/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332669"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332644/field-1/8ede37cdc50953654d22b24b9e75f8dd87d0e058b318caee7f92e51df02c163b" :answer/id #uuid "99e462e1-4c24-599b-9784-bc6a819f34bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}e^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332644/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b442ff4e-d61d-572e-aa1f-a0e72d9b120b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332644/field-1/8ede37cdc50953654d22b24b9e75f8dd87d0e058b318caee7f92e51df02c163b" :answer-field/choices ["q-332644/field-1/8ede37cdc50953654d22b24b9e75f8dd87d0e058b318caee7f92e51df02c163b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332644" :question/id #uuid "8903add6-0b71-512c-a68b-c77444eff598" :question/math-academy-id "q-332644" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=9{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (12-6)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332644/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332644"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332668/field-1/83781fd24d709546676f20e3adce38da772b9753d8cb69d0c209154ae293c4b0" :answer/id #uuid "0086491b-8f28-5d3f-b14a-87b1827fd9ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}e^{x}-\\frac{4}{21}e^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186301947 :answer-field/choices ["q-332668/field-1/83781fd24d709546676f20e3adce38da772b9753d8cb69d0c209154ae293c4b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332848/field-1/abac490e4a103d080c08c3af0cc459e72e302de5e58c218847178c1562250f55" :answer/id #uuid "1e0c4c76-5dfc-5f85-8d61-d54e89d0b5c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12xe^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}e^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332848/field-1/509b3c3d953718217239220e6f14d24f8e9c929473c7fa299e58169cab9feef3" :answer/id #uuid "28b42f88-262c-5a8a-b682-d6673316f11e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12xe^{-4x}-\\frac{8}{5}e^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332848/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a94788-eef2-5bc4-a13f-df483b975efc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332848/field-1/abac490e4a103d080c08c3af0cc459e72e302de5e58c218847178c1562250f55" :answer-field/choices ["q-332848/field-1/abac490e4a103d080c08c3af0cc459e72e302de5e58c218847178c1562250f55" "q-332848/field-1/509b3c3d953718217239220e6f14d24f8e9c929473c7fa299e58169cab9feef3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332848" :question/id #uuid "3a0dd5a1-7136-5b94-ab63-02aa4707dd74" :question/math-academy-id "q-332848" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+4(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x}+(\\beta +4\\beta ){e}^{x} & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x}+5\\beta {e}^{x} & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-4x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =8,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332848/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332848"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332686/field-1/9938c300105a628be661deb8849b60cf31d4af41f9b5113897f32442cc1e0882" :answer/id #uuid "0b39559e-dadc-5abc-a8e3-a5022294e4a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8xe^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332686/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a57619ce-03f2-5496-8140-e8c0883afbc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332686/field-1/9938c300105a628be661deb8849b60cf31d4af41f9b5113897f32442cc1e0882" :answer-field/choices ["q-332686/field-1/9938c300105a628be661deb8849b60cf31d4af41f9b5113897f32442cc1e0882"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332686" :question/id #uuid "9b63cbf2-f8fc-50ae-8f0d-7e9e3508d562" :question/math-academy-id "q-332686" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=8{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-4x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x})+4\\alpha x{e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332686/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332686"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332398/field-1/8b472729a6023a86fd21b2080520faaf08444c315176c7eec39934078ed2d5f1" :answer/id #uuid "7d54c2b7-2fd4-5ac6-9b17-e3e1e75fe048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6e^{-3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228015 :answer-field/choices ["q-332398/field-1/8b472729a6023a86fd21b2080520faaf08444c315176c7eec39934078ed2d5f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332398"] :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3\\alpha x{e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-333176/field-1/9d1d99fca219c0c6e20a6f97ba77d21f826e700ef0ee9fcdb394c3808c968310" :answer/id #uuid "8172e3fa-619d-5db8-b804-f1808d945c3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{5x}-e^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333176/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cfda6193-452b-5d2b-8c25-5d33498e04d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333176/field-1/9d1d99fca219c0c6e20a6f97ba77d21f826e700ef0ee9fcdb394c3808c968310" :answer-field/choices ["q-333176/field-1/9d1d99fca219c0c6e20a6f97ba77d21f826e700ef0ee9fcdb394c3808c968310"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333176" :question/id #uuid "2ebb7209-c4d1-5ca8-9307-dd4f5c48183e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333176" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=2{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha {e}^{3x}-5\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =2{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}-{e}^{3x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333176/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333176"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332895/field-1/33709432f98697b1ebc65fb5fdfc6053521e643f9114067f2656d5ae88f28869" :answer/id #uuid "50b49da0-5c75-570b-8b8e-d5c171bbfa66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{2x}-5xe^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186301970 :answer-field/choices ["q-332895/field-1/33709432f98697b1ebc65fb5fdfc6053521e643f9114067f2656d5ae88f28869"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332801/field-1/8ccad1a7d7bd7b1be28aed6fd71c632c6998eaee67a23a6669de231b7e170dd5" :answer/id #uuid "cee59641-63fe-5422-ac53-afc2d3bf4ffc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{x}+5e^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332801/field-1/f7fe60f66864bebca453c99dfed146253b1b306c73d04d8824d3c236bd1887f2" :answer/id #uuid "8def97b9-9fed-5af8-9fd6-823fc752faa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{x}-5e^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332801/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "690db9fd-5d56-5bdb-8177-c16979aaa76a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332801/field-1/8ccad1a7d7bd7b1be28aed6fd71c632c6998eaee67a23a6669de231b7e170dd5" :answer-field/choices ["q-332801/field-1/8ccad1a7d7bd7b1be28aed6fd71c632c6998eaee67a23a6669de231b7e170dd5" "q-332801/field-1/f7fe60f66864bebca453c99dfed146253b1b306c73d04d8824d3c236bd1887f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332801" :question/id #uuid "cb257cc1-ec43-5317-b99b-94cdb5d3aa8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332801" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=20{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $20{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha {e}^{5x}-\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+5{e}^{5x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332801/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332801"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332531"] :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{2x}-\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+2{e}^{2x}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-332908/field-1/545464af3ed6853ef0ee2c305e40dd47c3bac87943ea0a08be7a277b23e4cef1" :answer/id #uuid "54b852c8-03bb-5bac-b58e-3d6ca9d1263d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}e^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}e^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332908/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8af76db-ff80-515a-9f26-3a346ee89b0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332908/field-1/545464af3ed6853ef0ee2c305e40dd47c3bac87943ea0a08be7a277b23e4cef1" :answer-field/choices ["q-332908/field-1/545464af3ed6853ef0ee2c305e40dd47c3bac87943ea0a08be7a277b23e4cef1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332908" :question/id #uuid "835ac161-f259-59c7-85ca-dd6f39fecf57" :question/math-academy-id "q-332908" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=10\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{4x}-\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =2{e}^{4x} \\\\ 3\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=10$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+\\frac{2}{3} & =10 \\\\ A & =\\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{28}{3}{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332908/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332908"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332940/field-1/bd402dffd30db2d005b2fae724d86aa9e26872e5356876a3e220d1d8a5e3a0b2" :answer/id #uuid "9b3c54d9-bc5d-583d-8987-a2cb5bbe62ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10xe^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332940/field-1/2fa6a445e86d28ee263715d9a92e96b3ac70053168364ef8bf643cb34f4c0e0f" :answer/id #uuid "0d2c35bf-27c4-5a62-b2cd-6d52b03bce70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10xe^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332940/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8b826b9-8f4c-5074-98ca-03a81079183c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332940/field-1/bd402dffd30db2d005b2fae724d86aa9e26872e5356876a3e220d1d8a5e3a0b2" :answer-field/choices ["q-332940/field-1/bd402dffd30db2d005b2fae724d86aa9e26872e5356876a3e220d1d8a5e3a0b2" "q-332940/field-1/2fa6a445e86d28ee263715d9a92e96b3ac70053168364ef8bf643cb34f4c0e0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332940" :question/id #uuid "6e7c5bfe-a697-5dc4-aa2f-68c1dae5b4f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332940" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=-10{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=-10{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-10{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2(\\alpha x{e}^{2x}) & =-10{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =-10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =-10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-10x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-10x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =0 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-10x{e}^{2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332940/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332940"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332937/field-1/13b960f2fa2c82aefc6b6c32be3d4b75d854befbdb9085c8753ae401060ac8a2" :answer/id #uuid "b85a9f66-3027-5f91-9326-7620d080a26c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{4x}-2xe^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332937/field-1/a39fd384b8fca293084c985c580bd6f895c4262b5dcafb3597c611a89b9f1691" :answer/id #uuid "4ce082ac-27c8-5218-b64d-66392d740147" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{4x}+2xe^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332937/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb0b9f0a-28e4-5022-9c32-cc06ecdfc3da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332937/field-1/13b960f2fa2c82aefc6b6c32be3d4b75d854befbdb9085c8753ae401060ac8a2" :answer-field/choices ["q-332937/field-1/13b960f2fa2c82aefc6b6c32be3d4b75d854befbdb9085c8753ae401060ac8a2" "q-332937/field-1/a39fd384b8fca293084c985c580bd6f895c4262b5dcafb3597c611a89b9f1691"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332937" :question/id #uuid "4ffa1d9c-35b7-510b-b9ab-e033fffbefc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332937" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-2{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-4(\\alpha x{e}^{4x}) & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ (1+4x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{4x} & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332937/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332937"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
